-- ============================================================================
-- PD7 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Production Networks, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE LINE, AND THE WELL ON ITS OWN. `pipeSchedule.js` is
-- a published table and two closed forms. Nothing in it iterates and nothing
-- in it knows there is a network. `buildNetwork` validates and indexes and
-- never computes a pressure. And one well on its own flowline against the
-- separator is a two node problem the solver finishes in a handful of steps
-- and that a single-well studio already gives you. That whole layer is
-- Associate, and THE TIER ENDS WHERE THE SECOND WELL ARRIVES.
--
-- THE SPLIT IS IN THE CODE AND NOT IN A SYLLABUS. Of the six things this
-- course covers, exactly one iterates. `solveNetwork` is that one, and
-- everything that needs the whole system at once is above this tier by
-- construction. A learner who finishes here can state every quantity a
-- gathering system has BEFORE anything is solved together, which is a real
-- and complete skill and is also, deliberately, the baseline the two tiers
-- above then destroy.
--
-- UNITS ARE NOT A HOUSE STYLE HERE, THEY ARE THE MODULE'S OWN RULE. "MASS IS
-- THE CURRENCY. Every flow in here is a mass rate, lb/d. Surface volumes do
-- not add across pressures and mixing two of them is a mistake that hides for
-- a long time." So every flow this tier reads is lb/d, pressure is psia and
-- never psig, a pressure difference and a wall rating are psi, and a turbulent
-- branch conductance in lb/d per root psi and a linear one in lb/d per psi are
-- NOT the same quantity and never compare.
--
-- FOUR THINGS THE TIER MUST NOT LET A LEARNER ASSUME.
--
--   THE PIPE TABLE'S ONLY SELF CHECK DOES NOT HOLD, AND THE GATE THAT STANDS
--   IN FOR IT IS FAR LOOSER THAN THE CLAIM. Every row carries the outside
--   diameter, the wall AND the bore even though the third is arithmetically
--   the first two, and the module header says that redundancy is the table's
--   only way of catching a transcription error. Run it as written, in double
--   precision, and one of the twelve published rows already fails: NPS 6
--   schedule 40 gives 6.0649999999999995 against a published 6.065, short by
--   8.8818e-16 in. The shipped gate does not use equality, it uses
--   toBeCloseTo at three decimals, which passes anything within 5e-4 in. That
--   band is 5.6e+11 times wider than the largest residual any row actually
--   has, and it admits a real transcription error larger than the redundancy
--   exists to catch. There is no Python oracle for `pipeSchedule.js`
--   anywhere, so that one assertion is the whole of what stands behind the
--   table. Expert m05 prices it; this tier is where a learner meets the idea
--   that a redundancy is only a check if something checks it.
--
--   A DESIGN FACTOR IS AN INPUT AND IS NEVER DEFAULTED. Barlow is
--   P = 2 S t f / D on the OUTSIDE diameter, and the module refuses to bury a
--   design factor because burying one would be pretending a jurisdiction.
--   Leave it out and what comes back is the bare hoop stress, which is not a
--   rating anybody may operate to. On the published gate line the two differ
--   by a factor of 1.3889, which is 1/0.72 and nothing else. An unknown grade
--   id resolves to NaN and never to a nearby grade, which is the same
--   discipline in a second place.
--
--   A FIXED DIAMETERS COUNT FOR A FITTING IS A FRICTION FACTOR IN DISGUISE.
--   The equivalence is L_eq = sum(K) D / f, and the friction factor belongs in
--   it because the equivalence is between a K loss and a length of PIPE, and a
--   length of pipe only costs what it costs once it knows its own friction
--   factor. On the published fitting set sum(K) is 1.5, so a rule that puts
--   that whole set at thirty diameters is assuming f = 0.05, and the same set
--   at f 0.018 is 83.333333 diameters. THE BIAS ALSO CHANGES SIGN WITH THE
--   READING: thirty diameters PER FITTING puts six fittings at 180 and
--   OVERSTATES, thirty applied once to the list UNDERSTATES by nearly three
--   times. Anything written on this line has to say which reading it is using.
--
--   AND A DRAWN ARROW IS NOT A FLOW DIRECTION. A branch's `from` and `to` are
--   a sign convention chosen before anybody solved anything. The three node
--   kinds are the whole vocabulary: a well injects and its pressure is
--   unknown, a junction injects nothing and everything that arrives leaves, a
--   sink has a FIXED pressure and takes whatever arrives. Anything a real
--   gathering system has that is not one of those three has to be written as a
--   branch relation or left out, and Section 24 of the digest is the list of
--   what that leaves out: the hydraulics themselves, the inflow, temperature,
--   slugging, holdup, every transient, compressibility along a branch, and any
--   equipment between nodes at all.
--
-- WHAT THE SOLO SOLVE ALREADY SHOWS, AND WHY IT IS WORTH GRADING. One well on
-- its own flowline is a real solve with a real answer: the wellhead sits where
-- the inflow curve meets what the line and the trunk will pass, and the rate
-- follows. Two wells of very different strength on the same system land at
-- very different wellhead pressures, and the tier ends knowing that both
-- numbers were produced against a separator pressure that was STATED. Every
-- single-well study anywhere is run against a wellhead pressure somebody typed
-- in. The Professional tier is what happens when nobody gets to type one.
--
-- AND THE ONE HABIT THIS TIER STARTS THAT THE COURSE IS REALLY ABOUT. A
-- CONVERGED FLAG IS NOT A RESULT. Wherever this tier prints an answer out of a
-- solve, it also says what was checked and what was not. On a two node network
-- there is nothing hiding, and saying so on the case where it is true is what
-- makes the sentence mean something two tiers later, where it is not.
--
-- The only numbers that travel from this tier to the next are the system's own
-- stated conditions: the separator pressure, the wells' Vogel constants, the
-- branch conductances and the line's own dimensions. The tier above restates
-- every one of them from scratch and builds its own coefficients.
-- ============================================================================

values ('network', 'beginner', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-a-gathering-system","title":"A Gathering System","lesson_keys":["l01-what-a-production-network-is","l02-what-these-engines-model","l03-what-they-refuse","l04-six-functions-one-answer"]},{"key":"m02-the-line-and-what-it-holds","title":"The Line and What It Holds","lesson_keys":["l01-a-pipe-schedule-is-a-table","l02-the-redundancy-that-checks-itself","l03-what-a-wall-can-hold","l04-the-design-factor-is-an-input","l05-what-a-rating-is-not"]},{"key":"m03-fittings-as-pipe","title":"Fittings as Pipe","lesson_keys":["l01-a-fitting-is-a-resistance","l02-equivalent-length","l03-why-the-friction-factor-belongs-in-it","l04-one-valve-against-all-the-others","l05-what-a-rule-of-thumb-assumes"]},{"key":"m04-nodes-and-branches","title":"Nodes and Branches","lesson_keys":["l01-three-node-kinds","l02-what-makes-a-drawing-a-network","l03-refusals-not-repairs","l04-what-an-index-is-for"]},{"key":"m05-one-well-on-its-own","title":"One Well on Its Own","lesson_keys":["l01-a-well-against-a-boundary","l02-the-inflow-curve","l03-the-drop-along-the-line","l04-where-the-wellhead-lands","l05-what-one-well-cannot-tell-you"]},{"key":"m06-the-associate-reading","title":"The Associate Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'network' and tier = 'beginner';

